# 25. Preliminary Policy Hypotheses

The baseline suggests several policy hypotheses for later evaluation.

### Hypothesis 1 — A common conservation information architecture could improve performance without centralizing conservation authority.

### Hypothesis 2 — Persistent identifiers may be among the highest-value interoperability investments.

### Hypothesis 3 — Monitoring should be designed around management decisions rather than data collection alone.

### Hypothesis 4 — Early-warning systems require predefined response pathways.

### Hypothesis 5 — Biological and functional indicators should receive greater emphasis than administrative activity metrics.

### Hypothesis 6 — Long-term monitoring should be treated as core infrastructure rather than discretionary project support.

### Hypothesis 7 — Data interoperability may sometimes provide greater marginal value than additional data collection.

### Hypothesis 8 — Conservation agencies should distinguish uncertainty from ignorance.

### Hypothesis 9 — AI should augment, rather than replace, validated scientific workflows.

### Hypothesis 10 — National conservation performance would improve if information systems were evaluated by whether information changes decisions and outcomes.

