# 26. National Conservation Information Principle

The evidence examined so far supports a broader principle:

> **The value of conservation information is determined not only by its accuracy, but by whether it can be transformed into usable knowledge, connected to appropriate decision authority, and fed back into subsequent management.**

This leads to a stronger systems-level conclusion:

> **Information should be treated as conservation infrastructure.**

A monitoring station, ecological database, long-term survey, standardized protocol, species identifier, remote-sensing archive, or scientific workforce can perform a role analogous to physical infrastructure.

The value may not be immediately visible.

But when that infrastructure disappears, decisions become less informed, trends become harder to detect, and institutional learning becomes weaker.

